--- how this was driven ---
Real product code under test: bin/fm-remote-job-lib.sh's fm_remote_job_stop_worker_tree,
invoked exactly as tests/fm-public-followup.test.sh:60-66 (pf_test_cleanup) invokes it
after reading remote-jobs/worker.pid. The supervisor/child process tree and the fixture
root are disposable stand-ins for the environment, not for the product.
CONTROL arm replays the pre-change teardown (`kill "$pid"` only): the detached supervisor
survives and restarts a serving child that writes 10 more lines into the fixture root while
rm -rf walks it. FIX arm resolves the worker process group and takes the whole tree down:
group gone, zero further writes.
Host note: this NixOS host has neither /bin/ps nor /usr/bin/ps, so
fm_remote_job_process_command (bin/fm-remote-job-lib.sh:982) cannot resolve the group leader
and the stop silently degrades to a lone-process kill. The run above therefore supplied
/usr/bin/ps inside an ephemeral user+mount namespace (`unshare -r -m`, tmpfs over /usr/bin,
symlink to the store ps) - no host filesystem change. CI's Ubuntu images ship /bin/ps, so the
group path is the one that runs there.
